WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.478 --> 00:00:14.480
Mr. Fogg and Aouda set out for a drive through the countryside around Singapore.

2
00:00:16.341 --> 00:00:22.385
Passepartout noticed Fix following them and laughed quietly at the detective's elaborate efforts to keep out of their

3
00:00:22.445 --> 00:00:27.668
sight. While they were gone, Passepartout went about his usual errands.

4
00:00:29.492 --> 00:00:32.074
At eleven o'clock, they were all back on board the Rangoon.

5
00:00:33.435 --> 00:00:35.356
The ship sailed out of Singapore harbor.

6
00:00:36.777 --> 00:00:42.420
In a few hours, the high mountains of Malacca, with their forests inhabited by beautifully furred tigers, were

7
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.141
lost to view.

8
00:00:44.461 --> 00:00:48.844
Phileas Fogg hoped they would reach Hong Kong in six days, arriving on November 5.

9
00:00:50.125 --> 00:00:54.728
A steamer for Yokohama left Hong Kong on November 6, and Fogg intended to be on it.

10
00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.421
The weather, which had been fine so far, changed soon after they left Singapore.

11
00:01:02.002 --> 00:01:05.062
The sea rolled heavily, and the wind rose almost to a storm.

12
00:01:06.943 --> 00:01:12.144
The voyage was rough, but luckily, the wind blew from the southwest, and the steamer sped forward.

13
00:01:13.945 --> 00:01:15.745
Passepartout was very impatient.

14
00:01:17.066 --> 00:01:21.607
"You are in a great hurry then," Fix said to him one day, "to reach Hong Kong?"

15
00:01:21.627 --> 00:01:24.048
"A very great hurry!"

16
00:01:24.368 --> 00:01:27.088
replied Passepartout.

17
00:01:27.108 --> 00:01:31.271
"Mr. Fogg, I suppose, is anxious to catch the steamer for Yokohama?"

18
00:01:31.632 --> 00:01:32.312
asked Fix.

19
00:01:32.332 --> 00:01:35.554
"Terribly anxious," said Passepartout.

20
00:01:35.574 --> 00:01:39.517
"You believe he is making a journey around the world then?"

21
00:01:39.997 --> 00:01:40.658
asked Fix.

22
00:01:40.678 --> 00:01:43.660
"Absolutely," said Passepartout.

23
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.121
"Don't you, Fix?"

24
00:01:46.122 --> 00:01:47.503
"Me?" asked Fix.

25
00:01:47.523 --> 00:01:49.704
"I don't believe a word of it."

26
00:01:49.724 --> 00:01:52.546
"You're a sly dog!"

27
00:01:52.987 --> 00:01:54.708
said Passepartout, winking at him.

28
00:01:56.128 --> 00:01:57.809
This expression disturbed Fix.

29
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.071
"Has the Frenchman guessed my real purpose?"

30
00:02:02.552 --> 00:02:03.152
he wondered.

31
00:02:04.513 --> 00:02:08.015
He was puzzled and, after excusing himself, descended to his cabin.

32
00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:13.978
Somehow, Fix realized, the Frenchman had found out that Fix was a detective.

33
00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:20.261
From the very beginning of their voyage, Phileas Fogg had been completely indifferent to his surroundings.

34
00:02:21.542 --> 00:02:23.603
Aouda's charm had failed to change Mr.

35
00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:24.864
Fogg's behavior one bit.

36
00:02:26.556 --> 00:02:28.717
Every day Passepartout was surprised by Mr.

37
00:02:28.757 --> 00:02:30.517
Fogg's cool manner toward Aouda.

38
00:02:31.558 --> 00:02:36.039
It was obvious to Passepartout that Aouda felt a deep gratitude to him for saving her life.

39
00:02:37.419 --> 00:02:40.980
Passepartout was beginning to think his master was quite heartless indeed.

40
00:02:42.441 --> 00:02:44.341
Aouda was also wondering how Mr.

41
00:02:44.381 --> 00:02:47.522
Fogg could remain so robot-like, even in her presence.

42
00:02:49.443 --> 00:02:52.964
As the Rangoon approached the end of its journey, the rough weather grew worse.

43
00:02:54.592 --> 00:02:58.795
The wind turned to blow from the northwest, and the steamer fell behind schedule.

44
00:03:00.356 --> 00:03:03.558
On November 3, a tempest tossed the steamer with a fury.

45
00:03:04.679 --> 00:03:08.641
The captain estimated that they would reach Hong Kong twenty hours behind schedule.

46
00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:14.505
Phileas Fogg stood at the ship's rail, gazing at the stormy sea with his usual tranquility.

47
00:03:16.487 --> 00:03:19.949
Aouda was amazed to find him as calm as the day she had first seen him.

48
00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:26.418
His expression didn't change, not for an instant, even though a delay of twenty hours would make him

49
00:03:26.478 --> 00:03:28.600
too late to catch the steamer to Yokohama.

50
00:03:30.561 --> 00:03:33.244
If he missed the steamer, he would likely lose his wager.

51
00:03:34.905 --> 00:03:36.486
Fix viewed the storm in a different light.

52
00:03:37.767 --> 00:03:40.510
Any delay would give the warrant more time to reach Hong Kong.

53
00:03:41.851 --> 00:03:42.391
And if Mr.

54
00:03:42.411 --> 00:03:46.975
Fogg missed the boat for Yokohama, he would be obliged to remain some days in Hong Kong.

55
00:03:48.416 --> 00:03:52.680
Passepartout, on the other hand, was enraged beyond expression by the difficult weather.

56
00:03:53.844 --> 00:03:55.705
Everything had gone so well until now!

57
00:03:56.985 --> 00:04:01.947
Throughout the worst of the wind, rain, and waves, he remained on deck, working with the crew, pitching

58
00:04:02.027 --> 00:04:05.047
in wherever he could to help the Rangoon's progress through the storm.

59
00:04:07.428 --> 00:04:12.470
On November 4 the sea calmed, the wind turned favorable, and the steamer made good progress again.

60
00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.511
Land was sighted on the morning of November 6.

61
00:04:17.751 --> 00:04:19.752
They were 24 hours behind schedule.

62
00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.323
A harbor captain sailed out to meet the Rangoon, to guide its captain through the channels and islands

63
00:04:26.383 --> 00:04:27.543
around the port of Hong Kong.

64
00:04:29.505 --> 00:04:34.247
Mr. Fogg did not hesitate to approach the harbor captain and ask about the steamer to Yokohama.

65
00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:39.490
"The Carnatic leaves at high tide tomorrow morning," said the captain.

66
00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:45.333
"The steamer had to have one of its boilers repaired so its departure was postponed until tomorrow."

67
00:04:45.353 --> 00:04:49.436
"Ah, thank you," said Mr.

68
00:04:49.456 --> 00:04:51.729
Fogg, without betraying any astonishment.

69
00:04:53.311 --> 00:04:55.432
At one o'clock the Rangoon was at the quay.

70
00:04:56.673 --> 00:05:00.476
Mr. Fogg led Aouda to the Club Hotel and reserved a room for the young woman.

71
00:05:02.317 --> 00:05:07.121
After seeing that she had everything she needed, he set out in search of her wealthy relative, Jeejeeh.

72
00:05:08.642 --> 00:05:09.063
When Mr.

73
00:05:09.103 --> 00:05:11.945
Fogg returned to the hotel, he told her what he had learned.

74
00:05:13.026 --> 00:05:15.808
Mr. Jeejeeh had left China two years earlier.

75
00:05:17.056 --> 00:05:20.619
He had retired with his immense fortune and taken up residence in Holland.

76
00:05:22.181 --> 00:05:25.583
In her sweet, soft voice, Aouda asked, "What should I do, Mr.

77
00:05:25.603 --> 00:05:28.586
Fogg?" "It is very simple," he said.

78
00:05:28.606 --> 00:05:31.308
"Come to Europe."

79
00:05:31.328 --> 00:05:32.349
Aouda was surprised.

80
00:05:32.369 --> 00:05:38.935
"But I cannot intrude—" "You do not intrude, nor do you delay, my project in the least.

81
00:05:40.196 --> 00:05:41.797
Passepartout!" he called.

82
00:05:41.817 --> 00:05:45.564
"Monsieur?" said Passepartout.

83
00:05:45.584 --> 00:05:49.086
"Go to the Carnatic and reserve three cabins."

84
00:05:51.827 --> 00:05:55.129
We have arrived in Hong Kong, and let me tell you, it is a bustling city!

85
00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:02.172
The name "Hong Kong" means "fragrant harbor," and its own harbor was once surrounded by flowering trees.

86
00:06:03.933 --> 00:06:10.216
Hong Kong consists of three main areas─Hong Kong Island, Kowloon Peninsula, and the New Territories.

87
00:06:11.388 --> 00:06:17.334
So Hong Kong is bigger than most people think, with 236 islands in total.

88
00:06:17.514 --> 00:06:21.858
In 1834 Britain attacked China and demanded the country give Hong Kong to Britain.

89
00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.463
China refused but did allow Britain to lease Hong Kong.

90
00:06:27.924 --> 00:06:29.265
I find that rather strange, don't you?

91
00:06:30.126 --> 00:06:32.108
A country being "rented" to another country?

92
00:06:33.449 --> 00:06:36.132
Maybe you're wondering why the British want Hong Kong for a colony.

93
00:06:37.152 --> 00:06:42.646
Well, it's mostly because Hong Kong is a massive trading port between East and West, and it acts

94
00:06:42.747 --> 00:06:46.295
as the gateway from America to Asia, and from Europe to Asia.
